course: history
lesson: 73
title: Cities: why they grow, and how water and sewers made them safe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Imagine your street with no bins, no drains and no taps: every bucket of dirty water poured out of the window, and the drinking water drawn from the same river; for most of history big cities were exactly like that, and many of them often saw more deaths than births.
explain: People still came, because cities had what villages lacked: jobs, markets, temples, schools, doctors and walls for safety; in Europa Universalis you just click «develop» on a province, but real cities grew where farms had extra food and traders met.
explain: Then, step by step, cities learned to keep people alive: a doctor in London mapped where people fell ill and found a poisoned water pump, an engineer built huge sewers after a summer called the Great Stink, and new rules followed fires and floods.
explain: Only in 2007 did more than half of all people live in cities, so the city world is very new, and about 2 billion people still have no safe drinking water at home.
explain: Your tap and your toilet are among the greatest life-savers ever invented, even if no game gives them a wonder.
fact: Cities grew where farms made extra food and where traders and rulers gathered; Uruk in Mesopotamia may have had tens of thousands of people about 5,000 years ago.
fact: For most of history only a small share of people lived in cities: around 1800 perhaps 1 person in 14 did.
fact: In 2007 more than half of the world's people lived in cities for the first time, and the share is still rising.
fact: In the hot summer of 1858 the River Thames in London smelled so bad from waste that it was called the Great Stink; the engineer Joseph Bazalgette then built a huge sewer system, mostly finished by the 1870s and still in use.
fact: In 1854 the doctor John Snow mapped cholera cases in London, found many around one water pump on Broad Street, and the pump handle was removed; his work helped show that dirty water, not bad air, spreads cholera.
fact: An early flush toilet was designed in England in 1596 by John Harington, but flush toilets became common in homes only in the 1800s and 1900s, and billions of people today still lack safe toilets.
fact: In old Edinburgh people sometimes threw waste from upstairs windows with the shout «gardyloo», probably from French for «watch out for the water»; it was against the rules, and towns fined people for it.
fact: In 1666 the Great Fire of London burned much of the City for four days; afterwards new buildings had to be of brick and stone, and fire insurance and fire brigades grew.
fact: Gas street lights first lit a London street in 1807, and electric street lights spread later in the 1800s.
fact: The London Underground opened in 1863 as the world's first underground railway; horse-drawn buses had begun in Paris in 1828.
fact: Central Park in New York was planned from 1858 to give crowded city people green space; it did not just happen.
fact: About 2 billion people in the world still have no safe drinking water at home, and girls often spend hours fetching water.
exercise: Why did people move from villages to cities? | Cities offered jobs, markets, temples, learning and safety | Cities had much cleaner air and water than villages ever did | Kings forced everyone to move there by law | Villages were banned by the first city rulers
exercise: Where did early cities grow? | Where farms made extra food and traders met | Only on the tops of the highest mountains | Only in deserts far away from any water | Wherever a king happened to throw a dice
exercise: Around 1800, about how many people lived in towns and cities? | About 1 in 14 | About 9 in 10 | About half | Nobody at all
exercise: When did more than half of all people first live in cities? | In 2007 | In 1066, under the Normans | In about 1800 | Back in Roman times
exercise: What was the Great Stink of 1858? | A summer when London's river smelled terribly of waste | A cheese festival in Paris that went on for a whole month | A volcano erupting near London and filling the air with smoke | A war fought with stink bombs between two London gangs
exercise: What did Joseph Bazalgette build after the Great Stink? | A huge sewer system for London | The first skyscraper in the City of London | The first line of the London Underground | The clock tower known as Big Ben
exercise: What did John Snow do in 1854? | He mapped cholera cases and traced them to one water pump | He invented the flush toilet and fitted one in every house | He discovered the first germ by looking through a telescope | He built all of London's sewers by himself
exercise: What did John Snow's map help to show? | Dirty water spreads cholera | Bad smells cause all diseases | Cholera spreads through cats | Pumps make people healthy
exercise: Why were flush toilets such a big step? | Keeping waste away from water cut disease, especially for children | They made houses look fancy, so rich people paid more for them | They used much less water than buckets did | They were cheaper than food, so everyone could get one
exercise: What did «gardyloo» warn people about in old Edinburgh? | Waste being thrown from a window above | A fire breaking out in the next street | The king riding past on his horse | Heavy rain coming down from the hills
exercise: What changed after the Great Fire of London in 1666? | New buildings had to be made of brick and stone | London was abandoned for ever and a new city was built | All new houses had to be made of straw and wood | Fires were made illegal everywhere in England
exercise: When did gas street lights first light a London street? | In 1807 | In 1066, for the king | In about 1950 | In 1666, after the fire
exercise: What was special about the London Underground in 1863? | It was the first underground railway in the world | It was the first car factory built under the ground | It ran on electricity right from the very first day | It was dug by the Romans and only reopened in 1863
exercise: Why was Central Park planned in 1858? | To give crowded city people green space | To grow wheat and potatoes for the city | It was never planned; it was always a wild forest | To build a royal palace for the king
exercise: About how many people still have no safe drinking water at home? | About 2 billion | Nobody | About 2,000 | Everyone outside Europe
test: Jaden: "Villages were chill tho, why'd anyone move to a stinky city?" ("Chill" means relaxed.) Best answer? | Cities had jobs, markets, temples and safety that villages lacked | Cities were cleaner and healthier than villages, so people lived longer there | People were not allowed to leave their villages | Nobody moved; cities just filled up with tourists
test: Aroha's Kiwi dad: "Yeah nah, most folks in cities is pretty recent, eh." ("Yeah nah" means no.) Is he right? | Yes, more than half of people lived in cities only from 2007 | Nah, most people already lived in cities back in the Stone Age | No, it happened in Roman times | No, most people still live on farms even today
test: In Europa Universalis, Kai clicks «develop» and his city grows in one second. What did real cities need? | Extra food from farms, trade, water and many years | One click from the ruler, just like in the game | Nothing at all; they grew on their own overnight | Only a big army to protect the walls from enemies
test: Ella: "John Snow? Like, the Game of Thrones guy?" Dad: "Different one, love." What did the real John Snow do? | He mapped cholera cases in 1854 and found a poisoned water pump | He guarded a giant ice wall north of London | He invented the first sewers in 1666, after the Great Fire of London | He found the first germ on the Moon
test: Charlie's mate: "Flush loos are ancient, the Romans had 'em in every house, no cap." What's more exact? | An early flush toilet came in 1596, and they became common in homes only in the 1800s and 1900s | Your mate's right: every Roman house had its own flush toilet, and nothing changed until today | Flush toilets were only invented in 2000 | Every single home on Earth has one today
test: On an Edinburgh ghost tour the guide yells "Gardyloo!" and says, "Aye, hen, folk chucked muck oot the windaes every night." ("Hen" means dear; "oot the windaes" means out of the windows.) What's the fair picture? | It happened, but it was against the rules and people were fined | Everyone did it all the time, and the town council said it was fine | It never happened; it's a story made up for ghost tours | Only the king was allowed to do it from his castle
test: Oscar's brother: "London just burnt down in 1666 and that was it, game over." What actually followed? | The city was rebuilt with brick and stone, and fire brigades and insurance grew | London was left empty for a hundred years while the ashes cooled | The fire lasted only ten minutes, so not much needed rebuilding | Fires were banned by law
test: Leah's grandad: "Back in my day, kiddo, streets were proper dark." Before 1807, how were most London streets at night? | Mostly dark, apart from lamps, candles and torches | Lit by rows of electric street lamps on every corner | Lit by bright neon signs over the shops | As bright as daytime
test: Ollie's big bro: "The Tube's older than your great-great-grandad, bruv." ("Bruv" means brother, mate.) Is he right? | Yes, it opened in 1863, over 160 years ago | No, it opened in 2000 | No, the Romans built it | No, it opened in 1990
test: Ava's friend: "Central Park was just a forest they forgot to cut down." What's true? | It was planned from 1858 to give the city green space | Your friend's right: it's an old forest that no one ever touched | It's a farm owned by the mayor of New York | It was made by beavers
test: Mila turns the tap and her teacher says, "Not everyone can do that." What would happen if a city had no clean water and no sewers? | Diseases like cholera would spread fast | People would be healthier than they are now | Nothing, because rain water is always clean | The city would grow faster than ever
test: Finn holds his nose by the Thames. "Was London always this whiffy?" ("Whiffy" means smelly.) Guide: "Worse in 1858!" Why did things get better? | Bazalgette's sewers carried waste away from the river in the city | The river was filled in with concrete, so there was nothing left to smell | Everyone moved out of London | People stopped going to the toilet
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the investigation undertaken by John Snow in the year eighteen hundred and fifty-four. | It assisted in demonstrating that contaminated water transmits cholera | It established that cholera is transmitted by foul air rising from the river | It resulted in the construction of the first flush toilet | It led to the abolition of all public water pumps in Europe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proportion of humanity residing in urban areas prior to the twenty-first century? | It was a minority, the majority being reached only in two thousand and seven | It was the overwhelming majority since antiquity, as the records of Rome attest | It was precisely one half from the year eighteen hundred | It was nil until the nineteenth century
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the works of Joseph Bazalgette. | He constructed an extensive sewer system in London following the Great Stink of eighteen hundred and fifty-eight | He designed the first underground railway in Paris | He extinguished the Great Fire of sixteen hundred and sixty-six | He planned Central Park in New York
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of persons who at present lack safe drinking water within the home. | Approximately two billion | Approximately two thousand persons | No persons whatsoever at the present time | Approximately two hundred million
